>At the top of page 826 you develop the idea that both r and p(hat) [pardon
>the crude notation] are unlikely to be large.  But of course as you say,
>they could be if u were small enough.  Then you take away that posibility by
>saying that if u were sufficiently small we'd expect obligate behavior.
>That seems to be your only reason for saying that r and p(hat) can't both be
>large: am I right?  

Right.

If you take the frequency of social learning (p hat) as a given, then
equation 5 expresses r as a function of u.  For phat = 0.9, that function
looks like this: 

       ......................................................................
       .                                                                    .
     0 .                                                                    .
     . .                                                                    .
     8 .                                                *   *   *   *   **  .
       .                                     *   *   *                      .
       .                         *   *   *                                  .
  r  0 .                 *   *                                              .
     . .             *                                                      .
     4 .         *                                                          .
       .      *                                                             .
     0 .                                                                    .
     . .  *                                                                 .
     0 ......................................................................
        0            10           20           30           40           50

                                         tau

where tau is the mean time between environmental changes.  You have to get
to rather long intervals between periods of environmental change before r
gets very large.

>If so this might be one interesting issue to follow up.  I think that
>obligate b(oops) behavior will probably take rather more than 162 generation
>to evolve.  Thus there might be some middle ground where change is pretty
>slow so that socially learned material is still useful, but not so slow that
>obligate adaptations take over.  

I don't think that this is a sound inference, though your conclusion may be
correct.  Even if it takes 1000 generations for individual learning to
evolve, it might turn out that individual learning is favored whenever the
mean time between environmental changes exceeds 25 generations.  Dave
Stephens has just written a paper on the rate at which individual learning
evolves.  I haven't read it yet, but will let you know if it sheds any light
on this subject.

>You may recall that I advocated a different way of modeling costs when you 
>were working out you model, just because I personnaly found it easier to 
>understand.  Now it turns out to back to haunt ME!  
>I think I might be able to figure out how to measure costs of one type of
>learning or another but how can we measure efficiencies?  [e.g., (1-c)] 

I think you suggested an additive model of costs and benefits.  I worked
that model through, and my memory is that I got results that behaved sort of
the same, but they were nowhere near as pretty.  That is the only reason
that I stayed with the multiplicative formulation.  My model is especially
strange because it has additive benefits but multiplicative costs.  Rob Boyd
thought it was pretty strange too.

But just now, I repeated the exercise, and now I wish I had taken your
advice.  We can make my model additive by by re-writing my equation 1 as

   w_I = w + b - C

where C = bc in my old notation.  Equation 2 gets an analogous
transformation, with a new paramter, S, which is equal to bs in the old
formulation.  Now write C/b for c, and S/b for s in equations 4 and 5, and
you have equivalent expressions for the model of additive fitness effects.
For example, 4 becomes

                 1 - C/b
        r  = -----------------
             (1 - u)(1 - S/b)

                 b - C
	   = ---------------
             (1 - u)(b - S)

which, isn't any worse than the original equation, and it shows that the
quantity that I called the "efficiency" is really the excess of benefit over
cost in the additive model.  I must have gotten mixed up when I did this
before in order to come out with the complicated, ugly forumla that I seem
to remember.  I wish I had published it this way!  

Anyway, what you want to estimate are b - C and b - S, where b is the
benefit of "correct" over random behavior, and C and S are the (additive)
costs of individual and social learning.

